  "articleBody": "- [ ] Concrete moisture test result — excessive moisture vapor causes bond failure and blistering under protective floor coatings.\n- [ ] List of surface cracks, spalls, and pitted spots — repairing structural slab defects beforehand ensures an even, durable finished layer.\n- [ ] Removal plan for old paint or sealers — incompatible coatings prevent new floor products from bonding properly.\n- [ ] Oil, wax, and grease stain locations — petroleum residues must be degreased completely before mechanical profiling or acid etching.\n- [ ] Garage temperature reading — application ranges require between 40°F and 90°F to guarantee proper product curing.\n- [ ] Slab cure date verification — fresh concrete pours require about 30 days of cure time before applying floor treatments.\n- [ ] Designated staging area for garage belongings — items must be cleared completely out of the space before work begins.\n- [ ] Return-to-service timeline plan — vehicle access typically requires 24 to 36 hours of cure time, or up to 120 hours in cold weather.\n\nHow to Use This Checklist:\nReview these concrete factors before your project kicks off so there are no surprises with adhesion or turnaround times. If your slab has severe spalling or persistent moisture, Abraham Becerra and the team at HH Remodeling LLC can handle the concrete preparation and coating application for your home. Call 303-505-0866 to discuss your garage project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
